<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">21 November in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">5 events that happened on 21 November, from 1620 to 1995.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1620</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Mayflower passengers sign the Mayflower Compact</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Anchored off Cape Cod, 41 male passengers of the Mayflower signed a compact binding themselves into a 'civil body politic'. The agreement became an early landmark of self-government by consent in English America.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1783</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Two Frenchmen make the first free flight in a balloon</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Pilâtre de Rozier and the Marquis d'Arlandes rose from the Bois de Boulogne in a Montgolfier hot-air balloon and drifted about 9 kilometres across Paris in 25 minutes, the first untethered human flight.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1877</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Edison announces his phonograph</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Thomas Edison announced his invention of the phonograph, a machine that recorded sound on tinfoil and played it back. It was the first device able to reproduce the human voice.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1920</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Bloody Sunday in Dublin</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Michael Collins's IRA squad killed 14 men, mostly British intelligence officers, in Dublin that morning. In the afternoon, Crown forces fired on the crowd at a Gaelic football match in Croke Park, killing 14 civilians.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1995</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Dayton Agreement is initialled, ending the Bosnian War</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After three weeks of talks at an air base in Ohio, the leaders of Bosnia, Croatia and Serbia initialled a peace agreement. The war had killed about 100,000 people, including some 8,000 at Srebrenica.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a></div></li></ol>
